A||||5||BN Boomer||Need to get rid of a wrecked 87 coupe. What parts should I keep?||||5||
Z||000000||BN Boomer||04-11-2000||04:39 PM||||Okay, guys, I wrecked my 87 Coupe a few weeks ago. On my work to work one night, I lost traction in a rainstorm while doing 65mph on I-5, spun around, slid across 3 lanes of traffic, and impacted a concrete barrier rear end first at nearly road speed. First things fist, I walked away from the wreckage with only minor aches and pains. The car is nearly a total loss, however. The only undamaged body panels are the hood, driver's fender, roof, and nose. The rear frame suffered massive damage, particularly on the drivers (left) side. The left wheel is hanging by nothing more that the strut and brake line. I have already purchased an 85 GT to replace this car. So far I have removed the healights and front wiring harness from the 87 to use in the 85. The wife is on my back to get rid of the 87 carcass, but I really don't want to send possibly usable parts to the scrapper. I'll definitely remove the brakes and calipers, but what else do you guys think I should save? The engine still starts and runs. The interior is so-so. I don't know how well the auto tranny faired. Maybe I'll have some pics of the car later today. I've got to clean out the garage. I've been having some strange thought of running my Chevy monster truck over the car, too, but only after stripping it down.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick||04-11-2000||06:11 PM||||<BR>Hey Boomer, glad to hear you're OK. Cars are disposable, people aren't.<P>On the subject of car disposal, I would probably keep everything that wasn't bent, folded, or mutilated. In your situation I guess it depends on your storage space and your wife's patience. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||04-11-2000||08:46 PM||||Keep the nose, its the same as the 88 formula and coupe.  I think I need a lower front air dam section (the black strip) if you are parting it out.<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-11-2000||09:14 PM||||If you absolutly need to scrap the motor, I'd be interested in what you want for the coil brick and the TBI unit. I want some spares off something I know ran. (The brick is held to the block by 3 bolts. The TBI is held down by the air cleaner studs. just cut the fuel lines.) I'd like the engine wiring harness if it's intact. (I've got the only 87 coupe in deleware I think.) The exhaust manifold if it isn't cracked. (I'd be suprised if it's not..... 87's with automatic nearly always are cracked.) I can get the brick, TBI, and manifold local off other cars but the wiring I can't get.<P>save the nose, especially the skirt if it's good. Those 87 noses are hard to find. at least around here. a good skirt is next to imposible to find. It doesn't weigh anything. throw it in the attic. It probly cost to much to ship east or I'd be interested in that too..... save the plastic mush block under it. I think it's also 87-88 only. This could be used to update the older cars as well I think.<P>basically what patric said.... especially interior parts that are good. The radio trim is a good one to save. those break easy. the center console.<P>if it's got the sunroof you can save the whole roof. you can always put it on another car without one or use it for parts when some dolt drop something on the GT if it's got the roof glass.(fairly easy to get off. pull the head liner to expose the bolts. Be carefull with the part that goes along the sides of the windshield. they break easy. you'll likely need to cut some sealer to free that area.)<P>IF the steering gear (rack unit) is in good shape save it.<P>Look for RPO code F41 and/or Y99 on the fender sticker. If it's got that then you can also save the springs. F41 is the stiffer spring kit. It will fit all other 84-87 I think. You probly don't need it in the GT but someone out there I'm sure does. I think the sway bar may also be thicker. I haven't checked.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||04-11-2000||09:35 PM||||Hey Boomer, glad you're OK. Just out of curiousity... did you ever get that motor installed and running?<P>I would save every part that isn't broken or welded down.<P>If you don't have any plans for the nose and if it's in perfect shape I would be interested. Mine is warped where it meets the hood.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||04-12-2000||08:32 PM||||Most of the stuff you need to keep has been already mentioned. If the engine is a 87 2.5, I won't mind nabbing it, but Washington is too far away for a road trip. Here's what I'd keep:<P>Undamaged body parts. Fill up a old bucket with torx head screws, plastic inner door fasteners, plastic clips that hold the door handle and lock linkage, and other misc. stuff. It sure saves trips to the boneyard. Undamaged body panels. Steering column if it's tilt. Seat covers if they're any good. Interior facias and trim plates. The whole dash if the corners are still good. The instrument cluster and all the gauges. You already got the headlight assemblies. The battery, ECM, and engine wiring harness. Parking brake cables if they're good (new ones are expensive). Brake calipers and rotors (can be used as future cores if nothing else). The radiator and those long coolant tubes. Any of the exhaust system that can be salvaged. Just remove the entire doors and keep them (never know when you'll need a window or other door stuff). Send me a couple of those special rear deck grille/louver hold down fasteners if you don't need them. Save the quarter windows if they're not cracked. Keep the wheels if they're alloy and the tires if they're good. That's a few of the things I thought of.||||reg||1
